@layer kp.register {
    /* @kp-soft/themes — css/shade-light-register.css
   The shade-light register, 5.0.0 [S48, LIFT_PLAN row 6]: the light half
   of the shade pair. Scoped to [data-theme='shade-light'] and inert
   elsewhere; opt-in, imported after themes.css. The approved concept demo
   ("Quiet Margin", 2026-09-08) is the reference for every value here; its
   own in-page comment carries the research sources, the hazards refused
   and the computed contrast pairs, and that comment is reproduced in
   themes/shade-light/anatomy.md rather than duplicated here.

   The idea in one line: soft elevation, layered surfaces, shadow doing
   the work colour usually does. Warm paper (background 94% lightness),
   a slightly lighter card (97%) and a near-white popover (99%), a
   blue-grey ink that stops well short of black, and one rule the demo
   states in its own words — "accents that stay plates, never words"
   (--accent never carries body text; DI4/DI9 read together for this
   theme). Every place this file layers a shadow uses the same three-step
   scale below, in the theme's own foreground token, because a shadow that
   used a colour of its own would be exactly the DI9 fault this package
   exists to prevent.

   Colour discipline (DI9): nothing here is a hex or a named colour, not
   even color-mix() with a keyword ground — the demo's own CSS reaches
   for color-mix(in srgb, var(--x) N%, black) twice, and every register
   in this package before this one reached for hsl(from var(--token) h s
   l / a) instead, so that is what this file does too: the visual result
   is identical, the value is still one token's own hue and saturation.
   Motion (DI5, DI7): every animation and transition below sits inside
   the no-preference guard; nothing loops, nothing blinks, and the
   busiest moment (four headline words landing at 70ms apart) is well
   under the three-per-second threshold — see the report below and
   reports/di5.md. */

    [data-theme='shade-light'] {
        /* The configuration surface [AR43], the demo's own values as
       defaults: 0.4 for the IntersectionObserver threshold (the demo's
       own `{ threshold: 0.4 }`), 220ms between one lede mark clearing
       and the next, 900ms before the first one starts, 90ms between one
       redaction clearing and the next, 70ms between one headline word
       landing and the next. */
        --kp-reveal-threshold: 0.4;
        --kp-reveal-stagger: 220ms;
        --kp-classified-delay: 900ms;
        --kp-redact-stagger: 90ms;
        --kp-word-stagger: 70ms;
        /* The reveals this theme performs [S45]: js/effects.js reads
       these and leaves a theme that declares none at rest. No arrival:
       the demo's DI5 table lists none, and anatomy.md records why. */
        --kp-reveal-headline: blur;
        --kp-reveal-emphasis: sweep;
        --kp-reveal-rule: draw;
        /* The shadow scale: one token (--foreground), three depths. A
       plain flat surface (card, alert, dossier, spec sheet) carries
       none of these, matching the demo's own restraint; a surface that
       floats above the page (a dropdown, a popover, a toast, a dialog)
       carries kp-shadow-2 or kp-shadow-3, matching the demo's nav menu
       and dialog exactly (its color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 30%,
       transparent) and 35% become the relative-colour alphas below). */
        --kp-shadow-1: 0 1px 2px hsl(from var(--foreground) h s l / 0.1);
        --kp-shadow-2: 0 8px 24px -8px hsl(from var(--foreground) h s l / 0.3);
        --kp-shadow-3: 0 20px 50px -12px hsl(from var(--foreground) h s l / 0.35);
        --kp-backdrop: hsl(from var(--foreground) h s l / 0.45);
        /* The one hover-darken the demo uses (its own
       color-mix(in srgb, var(--primary) 88%, black)), as a lightness
       step on the token instead of a mix toward a named colour. */
        --kp-primary-pressed: hsl(from var(--primary) h s calc(l - 9));
        /* The pointer is the light [scope-25, built at scope-101]. Two
       declarations, and neither is a mechanism of this register's own:
       `--kp-pointer: track` is the bus dark and titanium already arm, and
       `--kp-light: pointer` names which surfaces that bus lights. The
       module writes six numbers per lit element; every rule below carries
       the fallback the register painted before this (x 1, y 1, lift 1,
       near 0 — scope-12's fixed light at the top left), so a page with no
       pointer, no script or reduced motion is pixel for pixel what
       shipped. */
        --kp-pointer: track;
        --kp-light: pointer;
    }

    /* ── Type ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

    [data-theme='shade-light'] h1,
    [data-theme='shade-light'] h2,
    [data-theme='shade-light'] h3 {
        font-family: var(--theme-font-display);
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    /* No accent beyond the serif face itself: the demo's headline and
       section heading carry no shadow, no rule of their own beyond the
       drawn rule the reveal hook already answers — a quiet accent is
       this theme's answer, matching the default row of themes/hooks.json
       on purpose (anatomy.md records this as a considered choice, not an
       omission). */
    [data-theme='shade-light'] [data-kp-surface] h1 {
        letter-spacing: -0.015em;
        max-width: 20ch;
    }

    /* Microlabels: the tracked, uppercase mono pill the demo uses for the
       side note's index and the concept label — the same recipe, in the
       theme's own mono token and primary ink. */
